# Trivy vulnerability waivers.
#
# No waivers are currently active. The two historic entries
# (GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 / msgpack, CVE-2025-47273 / setuptools) became obsolete
# in v0.3.1, when pip was removed from the runtime image (see the Dockerfile).
#
# Root cause of those findings: Trivy parses the vendored pins in
# /usr/local/lib/python3.*/site-packages/pip/_vendor/vendor.txt, which lists
# build-time copies (msgpack, certifi, urllib3, setuptools) that ship inside the
# base image's pip and are never importable from the application venv
# (/app/.venv). Removing pip cleared all of them plus a ~7 MB layer, so the
# image scans clean at CRITICAL/HIGH.
#
# Debian findings are handled structurally instead of being waived: the runtime
# stage pins a current base digest and runs `apt-get upgrade` at build time
# (that is how libzstd1 CVE-2026-124851 was resolved).
#
# Before adding an ID below, verify the package is really installed in the venv:
#   docker run --rm <image> python -c \
#     "import importlib.metadata as m; print(m.version('<package>'))"
